WEBVTT
Kind: captions
Language: en

00:00:00.221 --> 00:00:02.691
- [Man] What is the one element
of traditional corporate culture

00:00:02.691 --> 00:00:06.379
you can erase that gets in the
way of developing startups?

00:00:06.379 --> 00:00:09.510
- [Gary] You know, politics.

00:00:09.510 --> 00:00:12.646
VaynerMedia in the last five
years, I've grown the company

00:00:12.646 --> 00:00:15.082
quite a bit so
we're very fortunate.

00:00:15.082 --> 00:00:19.286
Whether it is Under Armour,
Pepsi or Turner, GE we're

00:00:19.286 --> 00:00:21.355
working with the biggest brands
in the world and one thing

00:00:21.355 --> 00:00:24.625
that's holding them
all back is politics.

00:00:24.625 --> 00:00:29.429
The game is built where people's
vested interests are not aligned

00:00:29.429 --> 00:00:30.697
with the organization's.

00:00:30.697 --> 00:00:34.534
Their bonusing people and
they're were rewarding people on

00:00:34.534 --> 00:00:38.705
a very certain task that is
completely against investing

00:00:38.705 --> 00:00:40.607
three to five years from now.

00:00:40.607 --> 00:00:44.128
Every decision is predicated,
especially in big companies,

00:00:44.128 --> 00:00:45.746
on 90 day terms.

00:00:45.746 --> 00:00:48.415
Because you're in a Wall Street
world where every 90 days they

00:00:48.415 --> 00:00:51.585
judge you and all the money that
everybody has humans from the

00:00:51.585 --> 00:00:55.402
CEO down are involved in
is the actual stock price.

00:00:55.402 --> 00:00:58.091
And if the stock price
goes down people get sad.

00:00:58.091 --> 00:01:00.994
If you look at what Jeff Bezos
does and even Mark Zuckerberg to

00:01:00.994 --> 00:01:04.096
a degree but not as well as
Bezos in my opinion is they

00:01:04.096 --> 00:01:06.133
completely disregard 
Wall Street

00:01:06.133 --> 00:01:08.869
and they're willing
to have wild swings.

00:01:08.869 --> 00:01:10.128
But then you look up every three

00:01:10.128 --> 00:01:13.240
to five years and 
things are phenomenal.

00:01:13.907 --> 00:01:16.510
The thing that holds them back
is the scoring system they've

00:01:16.510 --> 00:01:19.980
created for themselves which
is they're in 90 day terms.

00:01:19.980 --> 00:01:23.951
And there's nothing that
makes sense to invest in startup

00:01:23.951 --> 00:01:26.733
culture, VR like Scoble just
showed you, no company's going

00:01:26.733 --> 00:01:30.090
to invest in that 'cause
that's 90 days away from today.

00:01:30.090 --> 00:01:31.692
That's not real in 90 days.

00:01:31.692 --> 00:01:34.528
That's real in two years,
five years, nine years.

00:01:34.528 --> 00:01:37.965
And so for me, a lot of
entrepreneurs and me, myself

00:01:37.965 --> 00:01:40.534
I've been successful because 
I mentioned earlier it's gonna

00:01:40.534 --> 00:01:43.170
probably end up being the theme
of this which is I plan on doing

00:01:43.170 --> 00:01:44.312
business for the next 40 years

00:01:44.312 --> 00:01:47.641
so why wouldn't 
I bring you value?

00:01:47.641 --> 00:01:50.808
Corporations are stuck they're
being financially rewarded in

00:01:50.808 --> 00:01:52.512
90 day windows which is the

00:01:52.512 --> 00:01:56.984
antithesis to investing in 
where the world's going.

00:01:58.051 --> 00:01:58.852
Shit is deep, right?

00:01:58.852 --> 00:02:01.262
(audience laughter)

00:02:01.262 --> 00:02:02.656
But it's just the truth.

00:02:02.656 --> 00:02:04.958
People in here and I saw people
shaking their heads and I love

00:02:04.958 --> 00:02:07.894
that I can see you guys the
people that have been in it they

00:02:07.894 --> 00:02:08.961
know this truth.

00:02:08.961 --> 00:02:12.132
Which is smart people may not
agree with what they're doing

00:02:12.132 --> 00:02:15.131
but they're doing it because
their money is tied up in that.

00:02:15.131 --> 00:02:17.237
I have clients tell me every day
that they wish they could take

00:02:17.237 --> 00:02:19.373
the money from a television
commercial and put into Facebook

00:02:19.373 --> 00:02:22.275
video 'cause they believe me but
they can't because the way it's

00:02:22.275 --> 00:02:25.145
scored inside their organization
they would be vulnerable if they

00:02:25.145 --> 00:02:28.382
ran Facebook over television
so if the brand goes down and

00:02:28.382 --> 00:02:31.644
doesn't do well as long as
they're running TV they're safe.

00:02:31.644 --> 00:02:34.821
But if there were to take a
chance and run television, run

00:02:34.821 --> 00:02:37.457
Facebook and the business went
down even if it had nothing to

00:02:37.457 --> 00:02:39.926
do with Facebook or TV part
they would be vulnerable to lose

00:02:39.926 --> 00:02:42.466
their job and I don't blame them.
They have mortgages,

00:02:42.466 --> 00:02:44.498
they have kids support, 
they have their lives.

00:02:44.498 --> 00:02:48.402
They're not being put in a
position to take risks whereas

00:02:48.402 --> 00:02:51.671
in my life, entrepreneur land,
everything on my shoulders,

00:02:51.671 --> 00:02:55.008
everything on me, I'm only
being rewarded to take risk.

00:02:57.044 --> 00:03:00.781
- So all that what the tipping
point for dollars to quit

00:03:00.781 --> 00:03:04.010
getting pissed way
into television?

00:03:05.390 --> 00:03:07.087
- Death.

00:03:07.087 --> 00:03:10.824
When these companies die,
guys, big companies die.

00:03:10.824 --> 00:03:13.093
There's a lot of people in this
room that remember Sears being

00:03:13.093 --> 00:03:14.795
number one retailer in America.

00:03:14.795 --> 00:03:17.798
There's a lot of people in
this room that remember tons of

00:03:17.798 --> 00:03:20.200
things being number one and
are no longer number one.

00:03:20.200 --> 00:03:22.903
Back to tech, we all remember
Yahoo being number one or

00:03:22.903 --> 00:03:26.039
Microsoft being
number one, IBM.

00:03:26.039 --> 00:03:27.374
This is what happens.

00:03:27.374 --> 00:03:30.977
Big companies go out of business
and then a new regime comes and

00:03:30.977 --> 00:03:33.513
they win because they took
advantage of the arbitrage of

00:03:33.513 --> 00:03:37.751
marketing or innovation that was
underpriced while the incumbent

00:03:37.751 --> 00:03:42.255
was spending money on overpriced
activities or infrastructure so

00:03:42.255 --> 00:03:44.691
this is just the way
capitalism has always been.

00:03:44.691 --> 00:03:46.524
I always laugh when people say,

00:03:46.524 --> 00:03:49.529
I'm not saying anything interesting.

00:03:49.529 --> 00:03:52.165
I may say it interesting with a
little bit of flair and a little

00:03:52.165 --> 00:03:54.734
big of Jersey and a little bit
of cursing but if you break down

00:03:54.734 --> 00:03:58.538
what I talk about, 
it's super tried and true.

00:03:58.538 --> 00:04:00.040
It's just super tried and true.

00:04:00.040 --> 00:04:02.876
When I predicted three years
ago that everybody would use

00:04:02.876 --> 00:04:05.512
Snapchat and
everyone's like no way.

00:04:05.512 --> 00:04:07.247
That was just me doing the
same thing doing what I do with

00:04:07.247 --> 00:04:10.484
Facebook eight years earlier,
right, which was when there's

00:04:10.484 --> 00:04:15.255
enough people of enough people,
if there's enough people, enough

00:04:15.255 --> 00:04:19.059
13 to 18-year-olds using the
same thing, if a platform ever

00:04:19.059 --> 00:04:22.963
wins an entire generation it
will drag everybody else down

00:04:22.963 --> 00:04:24.297
into it.

00:04:24.297 --> 00:04:27.200
And so Twitter
never did that, right?

00:04:27.200 --> 00:04:29.669
Other platforms never
did that but Snapchat did.

00:04:29.669 --> 00:04:32.939
Two-and-a-half, three years ago
every single 14 to 18-year-old

00:04:32.939 --> 00:04:35.609
was on it which meant which
it was just a matter of time.

00:04:35.609 --> 00:04:39.913
So I think I get a lot of credit
for common sense which I'll

00:04:39.913 --> 00:04:43.783
gladly take and keep giving it
to me but I don't think it's all

00:04:43.783 --> 00:04:45.085
that complicated sometimes.

00:04:45.085 --> 00:04:48.255
I think what I do well and what
allows me to sit here and have

00:04:48.255 --> 00:04:51.525
